\title{pgsbridge: intrinsic transferability of polygenic scores between populations}
\description{
A polygenic score built in one population usually predicts less accurately in
another.  This package separates the part of that loss which is fixed by
population history from the part better data and methods could still recover,
by computing the best accuracy any score could reach.
}
\details{
The best accuracy attainable in a population \code{S} is the share of its trait
variance that is genetic,

\verb{PA_S = 2 sigma_S^2 Phibar_S / (2 sigma_S^2 Phibar_S + tau_S^2)},

set by three quantities: the additive genetic variance \code{sigma_S^2} fixed by
the allele-frequency spectrum, the average self-kinship \code{Phibar_S} summarising
within-population structure, and the non-genetic variance \code{tau_S^2}.  Because
the optimal predictor is given rather than estimated, this is a ceiling that no
score built from data can exceed.  Intrinsic transferability from a discovery
population \code{A} to a target population \code{B} is the ratio of their ceilings,
\code{PA_B / PA_A}.

The functions fall into four groups.  \code{\link[=prediction_accuracy]{prediction_accuracy()}} and
\code{\link[=transferability]{transferability()}} evaluate the closed form, with
\code{\link[=additive_genetic_variance]{additive_genetic_variance()}}, \code{\link[=shared_effect_size]{shared_effect_size()}} and
\code{\link[=split_effect_sizes]{split_effect_sizes()}} supplying its inputs.  \code{\link[=simulate_trait]{simulate_trait()}} and
\code{\link[=empirical_transferability]{empirical_transferability()}} measure the same quantities from simulated
traits.  \code{\link[=load_variant_pools]{load_variant_pools()}} and \code{\link[=draw_causal_genotypes]{draw_causal_genotypes()}} read genotypes
from PLINK files without loading pools of millions of variants into memory.
\code{\link[=group_summary]{group_summary()}}, \code{\link[=plot_direction_pairs]{plot_direction_pairs()}} and \code{\link[=plot_rare_variant_sweep]{plot_rare_variant_sweep()}}
turn replicate output into the figures.
